WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:04.960
PAUL GRAY: In April of
1968, Martin Luther King

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.390
was assassinated.

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:08.500
The small number of
African-American students

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.737
created a Black
Student Union at MIT.

00:00:11.737 --> 00:00:13.570
SHIRLEY ANN JACKSON:
The Black Student Union

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:16.570
presented a list of demands
to the administration,

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:18.375
except we actually
called them proposals.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.780
JAMES M. TURNER:
We felt that MIT

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:28.340
could be significantly enhanced
by having more minority

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:28.930
students here.

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:31.430
SYLVESTER JAMES GATES, JR.:
Many people questioned our right

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:34.200
to be here at MIT.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.270
Many people questioned
our abilities

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:40.604
to succeed at the
academic endeavor.

00:00:40.604 --> 00:00:42.270
There were people who
questioned whether

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:46.260
African-American students
had the capacity to innovate

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:48.990
in intellectual fields.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.010
PAUL GRAY: We created
a summer program called

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:53.340
Project Interphase,
which still operates

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:56.550
and was staffed largely by
African-American graduate

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:57.179
students.

00:00:57.179 --> 00:00:58.720
JAMES M. TURNER: We
wanted to do what

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:02.545
we could to ease the
transition into MIT.

00:01:02.545 --> 00:01:03.920
SYLVESTER JAMES
GATES, JR.: I was

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:08.194
in the first Project Interphase,
which took place in 1969.

00:01:08.194 --> 00:01:09.610
JAMES M. TURNER:
Part of the thing

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:11.320
was to bring them
up to the campus

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.830
the summer before they would
start so they could learn

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:16.090
the campus, so they
could meet each other,

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:17.960
and so they would
come here and not feel

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.395
like they were on an island.

00:01:19.395 --> 00:01:20.770
SYLVESTER JAMES
GATES, JR.: I had

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:24.200
to learn how to study
and manage my time.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.850
All of that happened during
Project Interphase for me.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:28.725
PAUL GRAY: We taught
get-up-to-speed subjects

00:01:28.725 --> 00:01:30.429
in physics and math.

00:01:30.429 --> 00:01:32.845
There was a humanities program,
and there was an athletics

00:01:32.845 --> 00:01:34.054
and social program.

00:01:34.054 --> 00:01:35.720
REGINALD VAN LEE: You
weren't just being

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:37.770
taught by MIT professors.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:41.175
You were being taught by other
minority students, many of whom

00:01:41.175 --> 00:01:44.650
had gone through
Interphase that were peers,

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:46.192
but were role models
for you as well.

00:01:46.192 --> 00:01:47.691
SYLVESTER JAMES
GATES, JR.: A couple

00:01:47.691 --> 00:01:50.130
of our students in Project
Interphase who had not

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:53.080
had calculus in high school
came to Project Interphase

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:54.000
and learned calculus.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.049
And they placed out
of 1801 and 1802.

00:01:56.049 --> 00:01:57.840
REGINALD VAN LEE: And
with that launch pad,

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.470
it made it easier to
take a risk, to step out,

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:05.380
to have confidence, to be
courageous, all those things

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:08.569
that got me through MIT and
has gotten me through my life

00:02:08.569 --> 00:02:09.177
since.

00:02:09.177 --> 00:02:11.260
JAMES M. TURNER: So
Interphase was this experiment

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:15.410
that kind of said, you know,
yeah, these people can do this.

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:17.035
REGINALD VAN LEE:
The best way to solve

00:02:17.035 --> 00:02:20.300
a problem is to bring multiple
perspectives to that problem.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.530
And the best way to solve
the problems of the world

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:25.740
is to make sure that all the
people that touch that problem

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:28.710
are taken into account as
you drive to the solution.

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:32.420
SHIRLEY ANN JACKSON: Paul
created an amazing foundation.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.910
And so there wouldn't
be a real presence

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:37.302
if it weren't for what
went on during that period.

00:02:37.302 --> 00:02:39.010
PAUL GRAY: Maybe the
most important thing

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:40.630
I did around here.

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:43.560
REGINALD VAN LEE: I owe a large
portion of my success at MIT,

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.730
and I would even say
since MIT, to Interphase.

00:02:47.864 --> 00:02:49.780
SYLVESTER JAMES GATES,
JR.: Project Interphase

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:54.330
was the singular most
important academic experience

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.207
I ever had in my life.

00:02:56.207 --> 00:02:58.290
REGINALD VAN LEE: One could
hope that you wouldn't

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:01.480
need an Interphase anymore
because all the disparities are

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:03.750
gone, and we're
all very diverse,

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.430
and all the stakeholders
are engaged.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:06.930
But that's not the case.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:10.700
And so I think that the work
of Interphase is so important,

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:13.730
changing the lives of
students like myself,

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.760
contributing to the success
of MIT and the impact

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:19.760
and footprint that it's
making on society at large.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.250
So we'll go 45 more years until
we don't need to exist anymore.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:26.900
[MUSIC PLAYING]

